<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sol Plaatje</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1876">1876</a>–<a href="/years/1932">1932</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sol Plaatje was a South African journalist, writer and political leader, a founder and the first general secretary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-national-congress">South African Native National Congress</a> in 1912. He went with its delegation to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> to protest against the Natives Land Act and described the law's effects in Native Life in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> (1916). His Mhudi was one of the first novels in English by a black South African, and he translated Shakespeare into Setswana.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sol-plaatje.jpg" alt="Sol Plaatje" width="204" height="228" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sol_Plaatje_002.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Natives Land Act of 1913 confined African land ownership to reserves covering about 7 per cent of the country, raised to about 13 per cent by the Native Trust and Land Act of 1936; the writer <b>Sol Plaatje</b> condemned it in Native Life in South Africa (1916).</blockquote><a href="/empire/union-of-south-africa">Union of South Africa · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in the Orange Free State. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannesburg">Johannesburg</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/south-africa">South Africa</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-26.2,28.04&amp;year=1912" aria-label="Johannesburg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5290 3126 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Sol Plaatje"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5740 3396h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5740 3396h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=-26.2,28.04&amp;year=1912">What was here in 1912? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Awaking on Friday morning, June 20, 1913, the South African Native found himself, not actually a slave, but a pariah in the land of his birth.&#8221;</p><footer>Native Life in South Africa (1916), on the Natives' Land Act &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/1452/pg1452-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…under the cruel operation of the Natives' Land Act little children, whose only crime is that God did not make them white, are sometimes denied that right in their ancestral home.&#8221;</p><footer>Native Life in South Africa (1916), on a child buried by an evicted family &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/1452/pg1452-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;They had no right or title to the farm lands through which they trekked: they must keep to the public roads — the only places in the country open to the outcasts if they are possessed of a travelling permit.&#8221;</p><footer>Native Life in South Africa (1916), on families evicted under the Natives' Land Act &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/1452/pg1452-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>1 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Is it to be thought that God is using the South African Parliament to hound us out of our ancestral homes in order to quicken our pace heavenward? But go from where to heaven?&#8221;</p><footer>Native Life in South Africa (1916), on the Natives' Land Act &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/1452/pg1452-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 535 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
